campbell@coyote.cs.wmich.edu (Paul Campbell) writes:
{P} As I recall, Nethack already does its own internal caching, so to run
{P} another cache on top of it would actually be bad for performance..

Well that's kinda correct.

NetHack on the PC uses an overlay manager that was written to
replace the MSC stock overlay manager.  One of the things that this
"custom" overlay manager does is try to keep overlaid code in memory
as long as possible before freeing the memory for use by another
overlay.  This is done based on a least-recently-used (LRU) algorithm.

Most caching programs also use an LRU algorithm, so there really is no
benefit to using memory for the caching program.  It's actually better
to just turn the memory into EMS and let NetHack handle the management.

